Who reviewed what

The record of how this paper was reviewed is best understood as two review panels, one outside reader, and a provenance audit that ran alongside them. Each of these checked the manuscript at different times, with different tools, and with different blind spots. The honest way to describe the result is that no single one of them was sufficient on its own, but together they converged on the text now in front of you. The account below is limited to what is verifiable in the project record; where a check happened but its evidence is not preserved, that gap is named rather than filled.

The first panel consisted of three LLM reviewer agents, each written under a distinct persona: one focused on methods, one on economics, one on multi-agent systems. They were dispatched by the hub under briefs that the hub itself had written. The panel ran through five rounds of review. The whole exercise was not peer review in any formal sense: the reviewers were not independent of the institution that produced the paper, and they were not blind to one another. That limitation should be read into everything the panel found, and everything it failed to find. Notably, the later rounds of this panel, rounds three through five, ran through the reviewing instrument on the same free-lane worker that had drafted most of the paper. That particular detail matters for anyone trying to reproduce the conditions, because it means the later reviewer passes shared infrastructure and lineage with the writing itself. The transcripts of this panel are public and live under the reviews directory of the companion repository, so a reader who wants to see the exchanges directly can do so.

The second panel was structured differently. It used four reviewer personas plus a chair, where the chair belonged to a different model family than the reviewers. This panel was run by the author's own review tooling, and the briefs it followed were written by the author rather than by the hub. It also ran for five rounds, on 13 and 14 October 2026, and it reviewed five successive versions of the paper: v1.5, v1.6, v1.7.6, v1.7.7 and v1.8. A reader should note that this second panel is not documented in the companion repository the way the first panel is. Its transcripts are not in the companion repository, so anyone wanting to reproduce that review from the raw exchanges does not currently have that material available in the same place. This too was not peer review, for the same reasons: the reviewers were not independent of the institution, and they were not blind to one another.

In addition to the two panels there was an outside reader. This was a person who was not a member of either panel. They reviewed version v1.7.9 and sent back five findings. Of those five, four were applied and became version v1.8. The fifth, which proposed moving some reconstruction detail into a supplement, was deferred, meaning it was acknowledged but not acted on in that revision. The division into four applied and one deferred is a useful illustration of how external input entered the record: it was not wholesale adoption, but a judgment about which findings to act on immediately and which to hold.

What the first panel caught, taken finding by finding, gives a concrete sense of the panel's strengths. In round one, it caught a retracted statistic that was still live in the abstract; the number had been superseded elsewhere but had not been cleaned out of that prominent position. This is the kind of error that is easy to overlook precisely because it sits in the most-read sentence of the paper. In round three, reviewer A reported a turn count that had come from memory, and that count did not reproduce when the reviewer ran its own query against the same material. The discrepancy between what was remembered and what was actually produced under a fresh query is exactly the kind of thing a re-run exists to expose. In round four, two reviewers, A and B, found an unverified query anchor. The finding came about because the two of them re-ran the published query at the hub's instruction, and the re-run revealed that the anchor the paper depended on had not itself been verified. So across its rounds the first panel caught three distinct classes of problem: a stale statistic, a non-reproduced remembered figure, and an unverified query anchor found through re-execution.

But the same panel had a significant miss, and it is worth stating plainly because it defines the panel's limits. A fabricated worked example passed six reviewer passes before a hub-side record check caught it. Six passes is the running total of reviewer scrutiny that did not flag it. That the example was fabricated, and that so many passes of automated review did not catch it, is a sobering data point about what this kind of review can and cannot guarantee. It was a check on the hub's side, against the project's own records, that finally surfaced it, rather than any of the reviewer passes.

The second panel's contribution, viewed as a set of catches, shows a different pattern, one that has to do with numbers entering the manuscript after the fact. The panel supplied a mistaken Table 1 total, 860 where the correct value was 840, which the author transcribed into the paper. The same panel then caught that error one round later. In other words, the panel both introduced the wrong total, through transcription, and caught it on a subsequent pass. Beyond that, the panel supplied four further sentences that were themselves in error, and again it was the same panel that caught those, two rounds later. So the second panel's record is a mix of errors it introduced and then caught, and errors that entered the text and were cleaned up by the panel's own later rounds.

Finally, the provenance audit. Two incidents were caught by the hub's provenance audit, meaning its checking of the paper's origins and lineage against the project's records. Three hub-side number errors were caught by the reviewers, meaning the reviewers were the ones who flagged problems in numbers that had been produced on the hub's side of the work. And the audit missed those same three. The pairing of those two facts is the essential point: what the audit caught, the reviewers did not necessarily see, and what the reviewers caught, the audit missed. There is no overlap that makes one of them redundant. Put differently, the audit caught two incidents that the reviewer passes did not flag, and the reviewers caught three hub-side number errors that the audit did not surface. No single check, taken on its own, was sufficient to find everything that needed finding.

For a reader trying to reproduce the record, the summary that follows from all of this is straightforward and should be trusted as a practical guide rather than as praise for any one mechanism. There were two panels with different authorship of their briefs, different tooling, different model families, and different evidence trails; one outside human reader whose findings were partly applied and partly deferred; and a provenance audit that ran alongside everything. Each layer caught things the others missed, and each layer missed things another layer caught. If there is a working rule to take away, it is that these checks are complementary and that treating any single one of them as sufficient would be a mistake. The fabricated worked example that survived six reviewer passes is the clearest argument for never collapsing the layers into one.

Two gaps should be recorded honestly because the fact sheet does not supply them and they matter for full reproduction. The transcripts of the second panel are not in the companion repository, so the raw material for reproducing that panel's five rounds, on those five versions, across 13 and 14 October 2026, is not colocated with the first panel's public transcripts. And the account above describes what each panel caught overall, but it does not fully enumerate the reviewer rounds and versions spanned by the first panel beyond the note that rounds three through five ran on the instrument, or the precise mapping of findings to individual applied revisions beyond the outside reader's four-and-one split. Anyone who needs that level of granularity will have to go to the transcripts that are public and the records that exist, and will have to take the absent material as it is: missing rather than reconstructed.

